"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,175\nThe Learning Brain. Series 7: Digital Learning\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,391\nEpisode 5\n\n3\n00:00:07,471 --> 00:00:10,096\nQuestions inside the video\n\n4\n00:00:12,896 --> 00:00:15,171\nTwenty minutes into an online lecture,\n\n5\n00:00:15,387 --> 00:00:17,117\nwhere is your students' attention?\n\n6\n00:00:17,456 --> 00:00:19,324\nThe answer may surprise you.\n\n7\n00:00:19,494 --> 00:00:22,276\nIn 2013, Karl Szpunar,\n\n8\n00:00:22,561 --> 00:00:29,359\nNovall Khan and Daniel Schacter at Harvard had students watch a twenty one minute statistics lecture online,\n\n9\n00:00:29,565 --> 00:00:30,460\nin segments.\n\n10\n00:00:30,922 --> 00:00:34,630\nSome students answered short quiz questions after each segment.\n\n11\n00:00:35,059 --> 00:00:37,916\nOthers did not, or simply restudied.\n\n12\n00:00:38,086 --> 00:00:42,533\nAt random moments, students were asked whether their minds were wandering.\n\n13\n00:00:42,987 --> 00:00:46,926\nWithout the quizzes, they said yes about forty percent of the time.\n\n14\n00:00:47,387 --> 00:00:50,488\nWith the quizzes, it fell to about twenty percent.\n\n15\n00:00:50,658 --> 00:00:54,586\nThe quizzed students also took about three times as many notes,\n\n16\n00:00:54,827 --> 00:00:57,026\nremembered more on the final test,\n\n17\n00:00:57,252 --> 00:00:58,944\nand felt less anxious about it.\n\n18\n00:00:59,472 --> 00:01:01,987\nRestudying the material, without questions,\n\n19\n00:01:02,239 --> 00:01:04,503\ndid not produce the same benefits.\n\n20\n00:01:04,673 --> 00:01:06,471\nWhy would a quiz help attention?\n\n21\n00:01:06,885 --> 00:01:11,252\nKnowing a question is coming gives students a reason to stay with the material.\n\n22\n00:01:11,637 --> 00:01:14,454\nAnd answering is itself retrieval practice,\n\n23\n00:01:14,644 --> 00:01:17,884\nwhich strengthens memory, as we saw in Series two.\n\n24\n00:01:18,434 --> 00:01:19,927\nIt also shows students,\n\n25\n00:01:19,947 --> 00:01:22,681\nand you, what has not been understood yet.\n\n26\n00:01:22,851 --> 00:01:23,829\nSome caution.\n\n27\n00:01:24,267 --> 00:01:26,349\nThis was one lecture on one topic,\n\n28\n00:01:26,369 --> 00:01:27,108\nin a lab.\n\n29\n00:01:27,506 --> 00:01:33,478\nThe authors themselves noted it is not yet clear how well the benefit holds across a whole course,\n\n30\n00:01:33,589 --> 00:01:35,432\nor in a live classroom.\n\n31\n00:01:35,856 --> 00:01:37,487\nIt is a promising finding,\n\n32\n00:01:37,609 --> 00:01:39,100\nnot a final answer.\n\n33\n00:01:39,270 --> 00:01:40,030\nFor teaching.\n\n34\n00:01:40,442 --> 00:01:44,305\nBreak videos into short segments with one or two questions between them.\n\n35\n00:01:44,632 --> 00:01:46,897\nKeep the questions low stakes and quick.\n\n36\n00:01:47,379 --> 00:01:51,275\nUse them in live lectures too,\n\n37\n00:01:51,631 --> 00:01:53,883\nwith polls or a minute of writing.\n\n38\n00:01:53,928 --> 00:01:55,546\nAnd give quick feedback on the answers,\n\n39\n00:01:55,994 --> 00:01:57,821\nso mistakes get corrected.\n\n40\n00:01:58,085 --> 00:02:00,793\nOne tip to share with students: pause and quiz yourself every few minutes.\n\n41\n00:02:00,963 --> 00:02:03,417\nA question is a small promise of attention.\n\n42\n00:02:03,695 --> 00:02:04,812\nAsk often.\n\n"